.TH FERRY 1
.SH NAME
ferry \- run a program on another system, shipped to it by a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ferry
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
.B --
.I prog
[
.I arg ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ferry
runs
.I prog
on the system that
.I cmd
reaches, with its standard input and output
.IR ferry 's.
.I Cmd
is a command that, given one more argument,
.BR sh ,
runs a shell there on its own standard input and output:
.IP
.EX
ferry ssh build1 -- u9cpu -n x
ferry docker exec -i app -- u9cpu -n x
.EE
.PP
The shell gives the system's name and processor, as
.B uname -s
and
.B uname -m
do, and
.I ferry
finds
.I prog
for them in
.BI /sys/lib/ferry/ os / cputype /bin\f1,
.I os
and
.I cputype
in Plan 9's names:
.BR linux ,
.BR darwin
or
.BR freebsd ,
and
.BR amd64 ,
.B arm64
or
.BR 386 .
The other system keeps the program in
.BR ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/p9web ,
named by its SHA-256 hash, and
.I ferry
sends it only if it is not there.
Then the shell runs it, with the
.IR arg s,
and
.I ferry
passes on what it reads and writes until either side ends.
.PP
.I Ferry
knows nothing of the programs it ships.
.IR Cpu (1)
runs its transport by
.I ferry
to ship
.I u9cpu
to a host.
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/lib/ferry/os/cputype/bin
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ferry/ os / cputype /bin
the programs
.I ferry
ships, bound there by packages: the
.B u9cpu
package's for
.IR u9cpu (1)
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/ferry.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR u9cpu (1),
.IR pkg (1)
.SH BUGS
The other system needs
.IR sh ,
.IR uname ,
.IR head
.B -c
and a writable home or
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME .
